1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,723
Aarav builds an amplifier for the fest.

2
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:04,814
In the morning, it sounds perfect.

3
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:08,847
By evening the room is hot, and
the music slowly gets quieter.

4
00:00:09,147 --> 00:00:11,394
A friend swaps in a new transistor.

5
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:13,260
Now it is suddenly too loud.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,553
How can an amplifier stay steady
when its parts keep changing?

7
00:00:17,553 --> 00:00:18,817
Negative feedback.

8
00:00:19,317 --> 00:00:21,720
Think of negative feedback as a trade.

9
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:23,557
We give up some gain.

10
00:00:23,557 --> 00:00:25,425
Gain is the amount of boost.

11
00:00:25,775 --> 00:00:27,745
In return, we buy good things.

12
00:00:28,095 --> 00:00:28,995
A steady gain.

13
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:30,130
A wider range of frequencies.

14
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,520
Less distortion and less noise.

15
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:35,425
And better input and output impedance.

16
00:00:35,425 --> 00:00:36,840
Let's see each one.

17
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:39,413
Let's test it on the breadboard.

18
00:00:39,763 --> 00:00:43,847
Here is our amplifier, with an
open loop gain of one thousand.

19
00:00:44,197 --> 00:00:45,671
First, no feedback.

20
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:48,082
The output is a big, strong wave.

21
00:00:48,432 --> 00:00:50,066
Now the room heats up.

22
00:00:50,066 --> 00:00:52,552
The transistor changes, and A falls.

23
00:00:52,902 --> 00:00:54,609
A drops to five hundred.

24
00:00:54,609 --> 00:00:56,766
The output wave shrinks to half.

25
00:00:57,116 --> 00:00:58,442
That is a problem.

26
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:00,597
The music gets quieter by itself.

27
00:01:00,947 --> 00:01:05,558
Now we switch on negative feedback,
with beta equal to zero point one.

28
00:01:05,908 --> 00:01:07,699
The output is smaller now.

29
00:01:07,699 --> 00:01:09,297
The gain is about ten.

30
00:01:09,647 --> 00:01:10,885
Heat it up again.

31
00:01:10,885 --> 00:01:13,500
A falls from one thousand to five hundred.

32
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:15,677
But the output hardly moves.

33
00:01:15,677 --> 00:01:18,872
A f goes from nine point
nine to nine point eight.

34
00:01:19,222 --> 00:01:22,209
Now the gain depends on beta,
which is just two resistors.

35
00:01:22,209 --> 00:01:23,378
Resistors stay steady.

36
00:01:24,028 --> 00:01:25,447
Why does this happen?

37
00:01:25,447 --> 00:01:27,151
Look at the formula again.

38
00:01:27,501 --> 00:01:30,212
A f equals A, over one plus A beta.

39
00:01:30,912 --> 00:01:34,931
When A beta is much bigger than
one, the one hardly matters.

40
00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:38,136
So A f is almost A over A beta.

41
00:01:38,136 --> 00:01:39,903
That is one over beta.

42
00:01:40,603 --> 00:01:41,936
A has cancelled out.

43
00:01:41,936 --> 00:01:44,602
The gain no longer
depends on the transistor.

44
00:01:45,302 --> 00:01:48,784
For a small change in
A, there is a neat rule.

45
00:01:49,134 --> 00:01:53,932
The fractional change in A f equals
the fractional change in A, over one

46
00:01:53,932 --> 00:01:55,008
plus A beta.

47
00:01:55,708 --> 00:01:58,420
One plus A beta is called
the desensitivity factor.

48
00:01:58,420 --> 00:01:59,662
Bigger means steadier.

49
00:02:00,312 --> 00:02:03,454
Negative feedback also
widens the bandwidth.

50
00:02:03,804 --> 00:02:07,518
Bandwidth is the range of frequencies
the amplifier boosts evenly.

51
00:02:07,868 --> 00:02:11,692
Without feedback, the gain is
high, but the range is narrow.

52
00:02:12,042 --> 00:02:15,001
With feedback, the gain
drops by one plus A beta.

53
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:18,938
But the upper cut off frequency
rises by the same factor.

54
00:02:19,288 --> 00:02:22,679
And the lower cut off frequency
falls by the same factor.

55
00:02:23,029 --> 00:02:25,828
Gain times bandwidth stays about the same.

56
00:02:25,828 --> 00:02:27,597
We trade gain for range.

57
00:02:28,247 --> 00:02:30,469
Feedback also cleans the signal.

58
00:02:30,819 --> 00:02:35,666
Distortion and noise made inside the
amplifier shrink by one plus A beta.

59
00:02:36,016 --> 00:02:40,970
In voltage series feedback, the input
impedance rises by one plus A beta.

60
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,303
And the output impedance
falls by the same factor.

61
00:02:44,653 --> 00:02:47,839
A high input impedance takes
little from the source.

62
00:02:47,839 --> 00:02:50,594
A low output impedance
drives the load well.

63
00:02:51,244 --> 00:02:52,611
Give up some gain.

64
00:02:52,611 --> 00:02:55,288
Get a steady, wide and clean amplifier.

65
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:57,249
Worked example.

66
00:02:57,249 --> 00:03:00,774
An amplifier has an open
loop gain of one thousand.

67
00:03:01,124 --> 00:03:03,471
Beta is zero point zero one nine.

68
00:03:03,471 --> 00:03:06,384
The upper cut off is
twenty five kilohertz.

69
00:03:06,734 --> 00:03:08,653
First, one plus A beta.

70
00:03:08,653 --> 00:03:11,830
That is one plus
nineteen, which is twenty.

71
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:15,889
The gain with feedback is
one thousand over twenty.

72
00:03:15,889 --> 00:03:17,025
That is fifty.

73
00:03:17,725 --> 00:03:20,204
Now suppose A changes by ten percent.

74
00:03:20,554 --> 00:03:23,372
A f changes by ten percent over twenty.

75
00:03:23,372 --> 00:03:24,939
Only half a percent.

76
00:03:25,639 --> 00:03:29,876
The new upper cut off is twenty
five kilohertz, times twenty.

77
00:03:30,576 --> 00:03:32,679
That is five hundred kilohertz.

78
00:03:32,679 --> 00:03:34,061
Twenty times wider.

79
00:03:34,711 --> 00:03:36,173
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:36,523 --> 00:03:40,611
Many students think negative feedback
shrinks the bandwidth, because the

81
00:03:40,611 --> 00:03:41,511
gain falls.

82
00:03:41,826 --> 00:03:42,726
Wrong.

83
00:03:42,427 --> 00:03:46,441
The gain falls, but the bandwidth
widens by one plus A beta.

84
00:03:47,091 --> 00:03:52,136
And in voltage series feedback, the
input impedance goes up, not down.

85
00:03:52,486 --> 00:03:54,107
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:54,107 --> 00:03:55,967
In PUC board papers.

87
00:03:56,617 --> 00:04:01,049
List the effects of negative feedback,
derive gain stability, and solve

88
00:04:01,049 --> 00:04:02,361
bandwidth problems.

89
00:04:03,011 --> 00:04:03,911
Try this.

90
00:04:03,823 --> 00:04:08,979
An amplifier has a gain of five hundred
and an upper cut off of forty kilohertz.

91
00:04:08,979 --> 00:04:12,996
Negative feedback with beta zero
point zero one eight is added.

92
00:04:12,996 --> 00:04:15,500
Find the new gain and upper cut off.

93
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:21,800
B.

94
00:04:21,306 --> 00:04:24,307
A gain of fifty, and
four hundred kilohertz.

95
00:04:24,657 --> 00:04:28,395
A beta is five hundred times
zero point zero one eight.

96
00:04:28,395 --> 00:04:29,498
That is nine.

97
00:04:29,848 --> 00:04:31,688
One plus A beta is ten.

98
00:04:31,688 --> 00:04:35,515
So the gain is five hundred
over ten, which is fifty.

99
00:04:35,865 --> 00:04:38,122
The upper cut off rises ten times.

100
00:04:38,122 --> 00:04:40,942
Forty kilohertz becomes
four hundred kilohertz.

101
00:04:41,592 --> 00:04:43,138
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:46,338 --> 00:04:48,198
Next, the emitter follower.

103
00:04:48,198 --> 00:04:51,706
Why would anyone build an
amplifier with a gain of one?
